+# Cache catalog results in Redis
+
+For whoever changes catalog reads or operates larder. Redis holds disposable catalog results to reduce repeated
+item queries during Saturday mornings, when the pantry item page is slow.
+
+## Context
+
+The pantry item page is slow on Saturday mornings. The requested change places Redis in front of the item query,
+and Tom can run Redis on the server. The repository contains no query timings or execution plans, so the underlying
+cause has not been established. Earlier decisions favored fewer services: sessions stayed in Postgres, and Redis
+was retired after it evicted receipt jobs. Catalog caching must preserve browsing when Redis fails and must not
+move durable data out of Postgres.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Continue querying Postgres for every search**: adds no service, but leaves repeated reads on the reported slow
+  path. Query tuning remains possible once measurements exist.
+- **Cache in each web process**: adds no service, but duplicates caches across the web servers and processes.
+- **Redis for disposable catalog results**: chosen as requested, with Tom available to run the service and shared
+  results across the web servers.
+
+## Decision
+
+Use Redis only as an optional cache for catalog reads. Postgres remains authoritative; a failed cache read falls
+back to the existing query and a failed write returns its database result. The cache has a short expiry because
+the result includes stock. Sessions and background jobs retain their existing Postgres storage decisions.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Repeated searches can skip the database query. Tom must operate an additional service, and cached stock can lag
+behind Postgres. Eviction loses only a performance optimization; the next read repopulates it. The cache does not
+establish that the Saturday slowdown is resolved: deployment needs latency and query-volume measurements.
+See [catalog behavior and limits](../features/catalog.md) and [enabling or disabling the cache](../runbooks/catalog-cache.md).
